<div>
<h2 id="real-life-images">The Hisense A10 Just Got Its First Real-Life Look</h2>

<p>On August 9, 2026, Notebookcheck published real-life images of the <strong>Hisense A10</strong>, the Android e-ink phone that has been one of the most talked-about reading devices of the summer. A day later, Gadget Review followed with its own hands-on piece on <em>"the E-Ink phone that snaps on a color screen."</em> A month after the official reveal on July 11, the A10 is finally stepping out of press renders and into the real world.</p>

<p>Why should an EPUB-focused site care about a phone? Because the A10 is the most interesting <strong>EPUB reading device</strong> of 2026: a pocket-sized Android 16 phone with a paper-like E Ink display on the front, a detachable color LCD on the back, and full support for sideloaded reading apps. For anyone who has ever wished their phone could double as an e-reader — or their e-reader could double as a phone — this is the device that finally takes the idea seriously.</p>

<p>Here is what the Hisense A10 actually is, what its confirmed specs mean for reading EPUB files, how it compares to the Bigme Hibreak Dual 2 and the Boox lineup, and the honest picture of what it is like to get your library onto it.</p>

<h2 id="what-it-is">What the Hisense A10 Is</h2>

<p>Hisense teased the A10 in early June 2026 and officially revealed it on July 11. The headline feature is a dual-display design: a monochrome E Ink panel on the front for reading, plus a color LCD that attaches magnetically to the back and can be snapped off when you do not need it. Unlike the Bigme Hibreak Dual 2, whose secondary screen is built in, the A10's rear display is detachable — a genuinely different take on the two-screen e-ink phone.</p>

<p>According to the spec details reported by <a href="https://goodereader.com/blog/smartphones/hisense-a10-e-ink-smartphone-specs-revealed" rel="noopener">Good e-Reader</a> and <a href="https://liliputing.com/hisense-a10-is-an-e-ink-smartphone-with-a-detachable-lcd-screen-on-the-back/" rel="noopener">Liliputing</a> after the reveal, the confirmed hardware looks like this:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Spec</th><th>Hisense A10</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Front display</strong></td><td>6.13-inch E Ink Carta 1300 (monochrome)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Rear display</strong></td><td>Roughly 5-inch color LCD, magnetic and detachable (NFC / pogo-pin connection)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Processor</strong></td><td>Qualcomm QCM6690 "Dragonwing," 8 cores at 2.9 GHz</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Connectivity</strong></td><td>5G + 5G DSDA dual-SIM, Wi-Fi 7, Bluetooth 6.0, NFC</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Extras</strong></td><td>Wireless charging, IR blaster (phone doubles as a remote)</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>OS</strong></td><td>Android 16 — no Google Play, sideloading supported</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Price</strong></td><td>Estimated around $590; China launch first</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Battery capacity, RAM, and storage have not been officially confirmed yet, and the detachable LCD's own resolution is still unannounced. Treat those as open questions until Hisense publishes the full datasheet.</p>

<h2 id="why-epub">Why an E Ink Phone Is an EPUB Device</h2>

<p>E ink phones have been around for years, but they have always been a niche within a niche. The A10 is worth paying attention to because it combines the three things an EPUB reader actually needs:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A paper-like screen for long sessions.</strong> The 6.13-inch E Ink Carta 1300 front display is the same generation of panel used in dedicated e-readers. It is comfortable for hours of reading, works in direct sunlight, and sips battery. This is what makes the A10 a reading device rather than just another phone.</li>
  <li><strong>An open Android base.</strong> Unlike a Kindle, the A10 runs Android 16 and accepts sideloaded apps. That means you can install the reading app of your choice and open EPUB files directly, with no conversion gatekeeper in between. We covered the best options in our <a href="/blog/best-epub-reader-apps-2026/">roundup of EPUB reader apps</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>A color screen when you need one.</strong> The detachable LCD is for everything E Ink does poorly: book covers, comics, charts, maps, and web browsing. Snap it on for color content, snap it off for distraction-free reading.</li>
</ul>

<p>That combination puts the A10 in a small club with the other Android e-ink devices we have covered — the <a href="/blog/meebook-e6-android-ereader-2026/">Meebook E6</a>, the <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma</a>, and the <a href="/blog/onyx-boox-picco-pocket-epub-reader-2026/">Onyx Boox Picco</a> — with one difference: the A10 is a full phone, SIM slots and all, so it replaces your daily driver rather than riding in your bag next to it.</p>

<h2 id="google-play-caveat">The Google Play Caveat</h2>

<p>Here is the honest catch, and it is a big one: the A10 ships with <strong>Android 16 but without Google Play</strong>. Hisense's past e-ink phones have carried a small app store with a handful of Chinese apps — WeChat Reading is the example Good e-Reader cites — rather than the full Google ecosystem.</p>

<p>What that means in practice:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Sideloading is the primary path.</strong> The device supports installing APK files, which is how North American and European buyers are expected to load apps. This is exactly how many readers already use <a href="/blog/fbreader-readino-ebook-store-us-2026/">FBReader</a>, Moon+ Reader, or Lithium on non-Play devices.</li>
  <li><strong>Kindle and Kobo apps can be installed.</strong> Amazon distributes its Kindle APK outside Play, and Kobo's app is similarly sideloadable. Books you buy from those stores carry their DRM inside the app, so purchases keep working.</li>
  <li><strong>Updates are on you.</strong> Without Play, apps do not auto-update through Google's store; you update APKs manually or through an alternative storefront such as F-Droid.</li>
  <li><strong>Not everyone will be comfortable with this.</strong> If you have never sideloaded an app, the A10 will feel like a walled garden with the gate left open but the directions missing. It is manageable, but it is friction.</li>
</ul>

<p>This is the single biggest difference between the A10 and Boox or Meebook devices, which ship with Google Play preinstalled. Weigh it before you get excited about the price.</p>

<h2 id="workflow">How to Get EPUBs Onto the Hisense A10</h2>

<p>If you do take the plunge, the reading workflow is straightforward once you know the shape of it:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Make sure your files are EPUB.</strong> EPUB is the format that reflows and annotates best on any reader app. If your library is full of PDFs, run them through our free <a href="/blog/pdf-to-epub-free-conversion-2026/">PDF-to-EPUB converter</a> first — and if you are weighing formats, our <a href="/blog/epub-vs-pdf-vs-mobi-vs-azw3/">EPUB vs PDF vs MOBI vs AZW3</a> guide explains the trade-offs.</li>
  <li><strong>Organize and clean metadata in Calibre.</strong> Books with proper titles, covers, and series data look dramatically better in any library view. Calibre is free, cross-platform, and the standard tool for this — our <a href="/blog/calibre-library-management-guide-2026/">library management guide</a> walks through it, and <a href="https://calibre-ebook.com/" rel="noopener">Calibre's official site</a> has the download. Metadata cleanup is covered in <a href="/blog/calibre-edit-epub-metadata-2026/">our Calibre metadata tutorial</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Transfer the files.</strong> Plug the A10 into a computer over USB-C and copy EPUBs into a books folder (Android exposes the storage over MTP), or use cloud storage — Dropbox, Google Drive, or even email attachments all work because Android treats any EPUB as an openable file.</li>
  <li><strong>Open in your sideloaded reader app.</strong> Tap the file, choose your app, and read. Most apps let you set the books folder as a library source, so future transfers appear automatically. If you are migrating a Kindle library, the same <a href="/blog/migrate-kindle-library-to-epub/">migration steps</a> that apply to any new device apply here.</li>
</ol>

<p>One tip: because the A10 has no Play Store, install your reader app <em>before</em> you need it. Download the APK on a computer, transfer it over, and install — then the EPUB workflow above is smooth sailing.</p>

<h2 id="comparison">A10 vs Hibreak Dual 2 vs Boox Palma vs Meebook E6</h2>

<p>If you are comparing e-ink phones and pocket readers, the 2026 field looks like this:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th></th><th>Hisense A10</th><th>Bigme Hibreak Dual 2</th><th>Boox Palma</th><th>Meebook E6</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><strong>Form</strong></td><td>Phone + detachable LCD</td><td>Phone + built-in rear LCD</td><td>Pocket reader, no SIM</td><td>Budget reader, no SIM</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Front display</strong></td><td>6.13" E Ink Carta 1300</td><td>E Ink (80 Hz panel reported)</td><td>6.13" E Ink</td><td>6" E Ink</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Color screen</strong></td><td>Detachable ~5" LCD</td><td>Built-in LCD</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Google Play</strong></td><td>No (sideloading)</td><td>Reported Android 16</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Est. price</strong></td><td>~$590</td><td>Crowdfunding</td><td>Mid-range</td><td>Budget</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>One-device readers who sideload</td><td>Dual-screen enthusiasts</td><td>Pocket reading, no phone duties</td><td>Cheap entry to Android EPUB</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>For deeper looks at the Play-Store Android readers, see our <a href="/blog/boox-palma-epub-reading-2026/">Boox Palma review</a> and <a href="/blog/meebook-e6-android-ereader-2026/">Meebook E6 review</a>. If you want a dedicated reader rather than a phone, our <a href="/blog/best-ereader-2026-expert-roundup/">2026 e-reader expert roundup</a> covers the whole market, and <a href="/blog/kobo-vs-kindle-vs-pocketbook-2026/">Kobo vs Kindle vs PocketBook</a> sorts out the mainstream options.</p>

<h2 id="who-should-buy">Who Should Buy the A10 — and Who Should Not</h2>

<p>Let us be blunt about the realistic picture. The A10 launches in China first, likely in the next few months, and the ~$590 estimate is before importers add their markup for North America and Europe. You will pay more than $590, wait longer than the Chinese launch, and you will do your own app management without Google Play.</p>

<p>That makes the A10 a device for a specific reader: someone who wants <strong>one pocket device</strong> that is a phone in the morning and an E Ink EPUB reader at night, who is comfortable sideloading APKs, and who genuinely uses the color LCD for covers, comics, or maps. If that is you, the A10 is the most complete version of that idea on the market.</p>

<p>If you are not that person — if you just want to read EPUBs comfortably — the mainstream path is still better. A Kobo, Kindle, or PocketBook is cheaper, simpler, and purpose-built, and a Boox or Meebook gives you Android with Google Play for far less hassle. Our <a href="/blog/drm-free-epub-library-2026/">DRM-free library guide</a> and <a href="/blog/color-e-ink-readers-2026/">color e-ink roundup</a> cover those routes in detail.</p>

<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<section class="faq" itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">
  <div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question">
    <h3 itemprop="name">Does the Hisense A10 have Google Play?</h3>
    <div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
      <div itemprop="text">
        <p>No. The A10 runs Android 16 but ships without Google Play, like previous Hisense e-ink phones. It includes a small app store with Chinese apps, and it supports sideloaded APK files, which is how most readers in North America and Europe will install apps such as Kindle, Kobo, Moon+ Reader, or FBReader.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
