        <p>Yes for the core experience: the 6.13-inch E Ink Carta 1300 front display is a dedicated-e-reader-grade panel, and Android 16 with sideloaded apps means EPUB files open natively with no conversion. The main compromises are the missing Google Play store and the unconfirmed battery and memory specifications.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line</h2>

<p>The Hisense A10 is the most complete e-ink phone concept we have seen: a real phone, a real E Ink reading screen, and a detachable color display that solves the one thing e-ink phones always got wrong — color content. With Android 16 and sideloading, it is a genuine EPUB device, not a locked-down curiosity.</p>

<p>It is also a niche product with real friction: no Google Play, a China-first launch, an estimated $590 before importer markup, and unconfirmed battery and memory specs. For the reader who wants one pocket device that reads EPUBs beautifully, the A10 is the best reason yet to wait for the import. For everyone else, the mainstream e-readers remain the smarter buy — and your EPUB library, organized in Calibre and free of DRM, will be ready either way.</p>
